WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.840
"Ultra-Processed Foods:
Concerns, Controversies, and Exceptions"

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.120
And now for our final speaker of the conference.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.160
He is the founder of NutritionFacts.org,

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.640
which has over 2,000 educational videos

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.640
on pretty much any topic on&nbsp;
nutrition that you could want.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.160
And over the last couple of years,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.160
we’ve learned How Not to Die,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.680
How Not to Diet, How Not to Age.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.160
And today, he is answering a burning&nbsp;
question on everyone’s minds.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:34.960
And that is,

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.400
what is the role of ultra-processed&nbsp;
plant-based foods in our diet?

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.280
Ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.240
it is an absolute honor to&nbsp;
welcome our final speaker,

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.120
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.920
Hey, hey, hey! Welcome everyone.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.080
I love this conference so much.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.080
So honored to be here.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.880
Alright.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.520
There is no conference I’d rather&nbsp;
be premiering a new talk for,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.560
so let’s do this.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.200
Modern nutrition science began about a century ago

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.920
in the context of nutrient deficiency diseases.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.520
So, there were editorials with&nbsp;
titles like “Sugar as Food,”

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.040
heralding sugar as one of the&nbsp;
cheapest sources of calories.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.680
For a mere six cents, you could&nbsp;
buy three thousand calories.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.360
What a bargain!

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.960
But the Nutrient Deficiency era&nbsp;
gave way to the Dietary Excess era.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.320
No longer were we dying of nutrient&nbsp;
deficiency diseases, like scurvy,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.960
as much as we were dying from&nbsp;
nutrient excess diseases,

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.280
like obesity and heart disease.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.880
So, it became more about&nbsp;
avoiding too many calories,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.160
too much saturated fat,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.240
too much sugar, too much sodium,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.960
but either way still focused on nutrients.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:24.320
This allowed food companies&nbsp;
to get away with abominations

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.960
like frosting-filled cereal,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:33.200
because it was fortified with&nbsp;
twelve vitamins and minerals,

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.280
50% better than the measly eight&nbsp;
in Marshmallow Froot Loops.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.520
However, food, not nutrients, is&nbsp;
the fundamental unit in nutrition.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.280
And to their credit,

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.320
the field of nutrition started moving&nbsp;
toward a more holistic view itself.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.520
So, first generation dietary guidelines&nbsp;
emphasized individual nutrients

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.000
moved on to second generation&nbsp;
food-based dietary guidelines,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.360
which largely converged on encouraging&nbsp;
diets rich in vegetables, fruits,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.080
legumes—which are beans, split&nbsp;
peas, chickpeas, and lentils—

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:13.760
whole grains and nuts,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.520
but an area of emerging importance

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.600
was the degree of food processing.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.640
What if, when it comes to nutrition and health,

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.320
it’s not so much the food or the nutrients

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.480
as much as the level of processing?

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.840
For instance, a food-based dietary&nbsp;
guideline might say something like

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.520
“Eat more vegetable soup.” Great!

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.400
But there’s vegetable soup, and&nbsp;
then there’s vegetable soup.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.320
Are we talking a&nbsp;
clean-out-the-fridge vegetable soup?

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.280
A health-haloed quinoa and kale organic

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.440
with a heart-stopping 1,200 milligrams of sodium?

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.760
How about a vegetable soup with&nbsp;
more salt than there are vegetables?

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.320
Or a vegetable flavor soup that&nbsp;
has more artificial colors and MSG

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.040
than it has vegetables.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.880
All soup is not the same.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.160
The degree of processing matters.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.480
Ultra-processed foods are these&nbsp;
industrial formulations which,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.520
besides salt, sugar, oils, and fats,

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.160
include substances that are&nbsp;
really not used in cooking,

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.760
like added ﬂavors, colors, artificial sweeteners,

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.960
emulsiﬁers, and other additives&nbsp;
used to imitate real foods,

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.600
like a frosted grape Pop Tart

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.800
with more grapes on the front of the package

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.360
than are actually in it,

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.760
with less grapes than there are salt,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.440
but it may artificially taste like grapes

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.600
and look like grapes because of

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:01.040
one, two, three, four, five different food dyes.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.320
Simply put, ultra-processed foods are foods

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.920
that can’t be made in a home kitchen,

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.200
because they have been chemically&nbsp;
or physically transformed

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.280
using industrial processes.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.160
They typically contain little or no whole foods,

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.400
are ready-to-consume or heat up,

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.480
and are fatty, salty, or sugary

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.040
and depleted in dietary fiber and other nutrients.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.760
So, that’s like all the sweet, fatty, salty snacks

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.640
like potato chips, ice cream, soda, candy,

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.800
French fries, burgers, hot dogs,

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.160
chicken nuggets, fish sticks.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.640
Basically, everything in a box or a bag.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.000
Why not just call them “packaged foods”?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.520
Well, they were actually thinking about it,

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.440
but they were afraid that some consumers

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.280
would look at like a bag of apples

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:47.920
and get confused or something.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.520
But what is so revolutionary

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.640
about this concept of ultra-processed foods?

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:59.440
I mean, wasn’t fatty, salty,&nbsp;
sugary junk always a bad idea?

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:06.160
Why isn’t it enough to just tell&nbsp;
people to stay away from junk?

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:11.680
Because, Diet Coke.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:13.680
Diet soda is the perfect example

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.800
of why a new kind of term like&nbsp;
ultra-processed can be so useful.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.280
Because it has no calories.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.720
No fat. No sugar. So, no problem, right?

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.600
Well then, why is diet soda consumption&nbsp;
associated with premature death,

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.600
stroke, heart disease, hypertension,

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.240
diabetes, metabolic syndrome, leukemia?

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.640
And most studies controlled for body weight;

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:42.240
so, it’s not just because heavier&nbsp;
people are more likely to drink it.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.800
Health risks are not only related&nbsp;
to the nutritional quality

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.800
of ultra-processed foods but also&nbsp;
to the presence of additives.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.880
No apparent calories, fat, sugar, or salt,

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.160
but contains caramel color,

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.440
which results in the formation&nbsp;
of 4-methylimidazole,

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.440
which is a potential human carcinogen.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:08.240
And contains aspartame,

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:13.040
also recently classified as&nbsp;
possibly cancer-causing in humans

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.880
And contains phosphoric acid&nbsp;
which is a phosphate additive

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.440
that may be damaging our health as well.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.240
And contains a benzoate preservative.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.440
If you remove artificial colorings&nbsp;
and benzoate preservatives

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:26.880
from the diets of preschoolers

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.680
and then randomize them to be slipped a placebo

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.720
or a hidden cocktail of colors and benzoate,

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.520
there is a significant reduction&nbsp;
in hyperactive behavior

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.160
when they removed these compounds

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.720
and then a significant increase&nbsp;
in hyperactive behavior

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.320
when they got the colorings and benzoate

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:46.880
compared to getting the placebo.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:48.480
Now, of course, it could have been the colors,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.960
not the benzoate, but that’s one of the problems.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.240
As little as we know about the&nbsp;
effects of these individual additives,

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.920
we know even less what&nbsp;
combinations of them can do.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.960
There is a large body of evidence

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.240
suggesting toxicity from certain food colors,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.800
preservatives, sweeteners, and emulsifiers,

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.160
in part sometimes through our gut microbiome.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.840
Now, industry apologists argue

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.520
that the FDA carefully evaluates food additives

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:23.040
to make sure that they are&nbsp;
safe, until they’re not.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.440
In fact, to show the system works,

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:31.040
they cite the removal of six&nbsp;
carcinogenic artificial flavors in 2018.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.320
Artificial flavors that had&nbsp;
been in the food supply,

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.240
approved for safety, since 1964.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.720
So, we were exposed for more than&nbsp;
50 years before they were banned.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:48.960
And that’s their example of the system working!

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.280
Aspartame, NutraSweet, was officially recognized

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.240
as a potential human carcinogen in 2023,

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.280
42 years after the FDA’s&nbsp;
own public board of inquiry

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.040
opposed approval based on it&nbsp;
causing brain tumors in animals

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.720
in the industry’s own studies.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:12.560
This included several FDA scientists&nbsp;
who didn’t think it should be approved.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:15.040
The FDA Commissioner, however,

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.000
rejected these concerns and approved it anyway,

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.200
before leaving the agency to enjoy&nbsp;
a $1,000 a day consultancy position

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.120
with the aspartame company’s PR firm.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.760
And then, the FDA actually prevented&nbsp;
the National Toxicology Program

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:35.040
from doing further cancer testing.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.440
Meanwhile, literally tens of millions of pounds

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:43.360
made its way into the food supply.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.200
Remember when the food industry thought
partially hydrogenated oils was a good idea?

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:53.760
Let’s replace saturated fats with trans fats.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.480
Although many countries now restrict their use,

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.160
trans fats continue to kill an&nbsp;
estimated half-million people

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.520
around the world every year.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:07.040
Of course, saturated fat is also probably&nbsp;
killing hundreds of thousands a year,

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:12.560
but the point is that trans fats from&nbsp;
partially hydrogenated vegetable oil

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.720
killed people for decades before&nbsp;
there were any limits on it.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:20.320
The FDA didn’t ban it until 25 years

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.840
after the first solid evidence that it&nbsp;
increased the risk of heart disease,

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:27.360
and meanwhile, every single one of those years,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:31.680
trans fats were killing an&nbsp;
estimated 50,000 Americans a year.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.480
That is quite the death toll

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.840
that can be laid at the feet of&nbsp;
the ultra-processed food industry.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:42.720
But they originally thought it was safe!

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.120
That’s the problem.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.280
Any time a chemical company&nbsp;
comes up with a new preservative

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:51.520
or sweetener or artificial color,

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.680
we have no idea how it is going&nbsp;
to turn out decades later.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.840
So, we can start to see the value of&nbsp;
this ultra-processed food concept,

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:05.200
in which an entire category of products

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.040
is essentially presumed&nbsp;
guilty until proven innocent.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.040
This drives the food industry crazy,

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:13.440
but look at their track record,

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:19.920
look at the trail of bodies they’ve left behind.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.320
If additives are the problem, well then,

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.280
why not just stick to so-called clean label foods,

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:33.760
made with simple, recognizable ingredients,

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.240
no matter how the food is processed.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:40.160
But that’s assuming additives&nbsp;
are the only other reason

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:42.240
that ultra-processed foods may be unhealthy.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:44.640
Harmful additives are just one of many ways

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.440
ultra-processed foods have been linked to disease.

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:50.800
At least the food additives&nbsp;
are listed on the label;

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:52.560
so, we could avoid them if we wanted to.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:56.000
Unlisted are some of the sneakier ways

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.640
ultra-processed foods may be unhealthy.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.120
Harmful additives are just one of the ways

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:05.040
that ultra-processed foods&nbsp;
have been linked to disease.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:08.160
Like advanced glycation end&nbsp;
products (AGEs), for example.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.600
Increasing evidence has shown&nbsp;
that uptake of dietary AGEs,

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:12.960
advanced glycation end products,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:16.240
is closely related to the&nbsp;
occurrence of many chronic diseases:

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:18.320
diabetes, chronic kidney disease,

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.160
osteoporosis, and Alzheimer’s disease.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:24.320
In fact, it’s hard to find&nbsp;
a single age-related disease

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:27.920
not associated with AGEs.

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.200
Dietary AGEs are abundant&nbsp;
in highly processed food,

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.200
because thermal treatments are commonly used,

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:35.760
but thermal treatment just means heat;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:38.320
so, we can get AGEs from industrial processing

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.560
or just from home cooking.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.160
So, let’s look at some examples.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:47.920
Canned corn has 20 units of AGEs per serving,

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:53.200
compared to corn chips at 151&nbsp;
and corn pops cereal at 373.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:58.960
Rice only has 9, but rice crackers 275,

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:02.400
and Rice Krispies has 600.

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:04.880
So, you might be at the store

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:08.080
and see some a no-salt-added rice crackers

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:11.600
or a no-sugar-added puffed&nbsp;
rice cereal and be like,

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:16.880
“Oh, it’s just straight rice.
Perfect—one ingredient.”

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:19.440
But no, because of the processing,

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.840
it’s not just that one ingredient.

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:25.920
A boiled potato has 17 per serving,

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:30.640
potato chips 865, and fast-food fries over 1,500.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:35.520
So, ultra-processed plant foods can have&nbsp;
nearly 100 times more of these toxins

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:38.400
than minimally-processed plant foods.

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:42.960
But even raw, unprocessed animal foods

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:46.560
start out with high levels&nbsp;
and just go up from there.

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:51.120
Instead of the 10 or 20 in&nbsp;
cooked whole plant foods,

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:57.520
fish, poultry, and meat start out at&nbsp;
around 500 or more in their raw state

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:02.080
and then jump into the thousands once cooked,

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:05.520
which is still considered minimal processing,

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.480
though processed or ultra-processed meat

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:13.120
can exceed 10,000 per serving.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:16.560
Although these contaminants are not&nbsp;
limited to ultra-processed foods,

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:22.160
you can see how just considering foods&nbsp;
based on their listed ingredients

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.160
fails to capture how food processing&nbsp;
can sometimes transform food

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.640
at a molecular level.

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:32.480
So, it’s clear that just using&nbsp;
this kind of clean label strategy

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:36.160
of sticking to foods with simple&nbsp;
ingredient lists isn’t going to cut it,

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:40.240
since you’re never going to read&nbsp;
something like AGEs on a label.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:43.840
It’s the processing itself,
whether traditional or industrial,

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:46.880
that is affecting the healthfulness of the food.

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:50.320
In the latest dietary guidance document&nbsp;
from the American Heart Association,

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:52.960
they advise choosing minimally processed foods,

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:54.560
instead of ultra-processed foods.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.800
Now, of course, they’re not&nbsp;
telling people to eat a steak,

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:59.280
which is considered minimally processed,

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:00.320
because they also emphasize

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:03.840
that we should eat foods low in&nbsp;
saturated fat and cholesterol.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:08.320
But just like there can be unhealthy&nbsp;
unprocessed foods, like steak,

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:14.240
they suggest there may be some&nbsp;
healthy ultra-processed foods,

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:20.240
like what about, plant-based&nbsp;
milk and meat substitutes.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:22.720
Admonitions against the consumption of products

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.040
simply because they are classified&nbsp;
as ultra-processed foods

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:29.440
may impair society’s acceptance&nbsp;
of a plant-based diet—

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:33.360
thus preventing any related health&nbsp;
advantages from being realized.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:39.680
And indeed, that does appear&nbsp;
to be a significant reason

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:44.240
why many people avoid these products.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:45.760
Is that a good thing?

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:47.120
Is that a bad thing?

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:52.480
Are plant-based alternatives healthier
than conventional animal products?

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:55.440
Let’s find out.

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:59.040
Food Compass is a nutrient-profiling system

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:02.640
that rates foods on a scale from 1 to 100,

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:06.400
with 1 being least healthy&nbsp;
and 100 being most healthy.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.800
Eight thousand foods have been scored;

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:11.360
so, you can see where your favorite food falls.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:13.040
And when I have my laptop next to me,

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:14.800
I ask people to yell out their favorite foods,

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:16.720
and we look up to see what the score is,

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:22.240
and they’re all disappointed that their&nbsp;
raspberries are only 93 or something,

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:26.800
but unfortunately, laptop’s in the back.

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:30.560
Here are some examples of perfect 100 foods,

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.120
and foods that score the worst,

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:36.880
all the way from kale down to Coca-Cola.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:41.280
OK, so the main objective of&nbsp;
ultra-processed “fake” foods

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:42.880
is to imitate real foods.

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.800
Like strawberry Pop Tarts trying&nbsp;
to mimic real strawberries.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:51.920
When you start with a perfect-100&nbsp;
food like strawberries,

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:54.480
the only way to go is down.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:59.520
So, fruit is better than&nbsp;
fruity-shaped marshmallows.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.840
But when the “real” food is ultra-processed meat,

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:07.920
processed meat, or unprocessed meat, or dairy,

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:11.840
there is a lot of room for improvement.

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:16.400
Food Compass has scored nine&nbsp;
ultra-processed meatless alternatives.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:20.160
Let’s compare them to the closest&nbsp;
conventional meat matches.

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:23.520
In every single case, the&nbsp;
plant-based meat was rated higher,

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:25.840
healthier, and sometimes by a large margin.

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:28.400
Nearly all scored at least twice as high.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:33.360
Or three times the health score, seven times,

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:36.560
20 times, even 60-fold higher.

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:41.200
Other common nutrient scoring systems&nbsp;
all found the same general trend.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:45.680
Now, better than chicken would be chickpeas.

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:49.120
And better soybeans than soy burgers—

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:52.160
soybeans are another perfect-100 food,

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:58.240
but the choice on the Burger King&nbsp;
menu is between this and that,

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:04.080
not between this and that.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.200
(Though, of course, there is a third choice:

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:10.080
not ending up at Burger King.)

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:15.680
This was just a few products&nbsp;
though, just as an illustration.

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:19.040
A 2024 systematic review identified nine studies

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:24.640
using nutritional scoring for the&nbsp;
assessment of over 2,000 meat alternatives

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:25.920
and their meaty matches,

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:28.160
and all of the studies, nine out of nine,

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:29.680
sometimes found comparable scores,

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:33.280
but mostly showed meat alternatives score better.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:34.960
Based on their nutrient profiles,

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:39.760
plant-based meats and milks would be&nbsp;
expected to reduce chronic disease risk.

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:43.680
When it comes to heart&nbsp;
disease, stroke, and cancer,

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:46.720
plant-based meats would be&nbsp;
expected to decrease risk

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.840
of these killer diseases by about 3%

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:52.640
per daily serving replacement.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:57.440
Now, whole soy foods might&nbsp;
take that down an extra 2%,

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:00.240
because they’re not weighed&nbsp;
down by the sodium issue,

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:03.440
but overall, swapping in even just one serving

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:05.200
of plant-based meat a day nationwide

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:09.840
could potentially prevent more than&nbsp;
100,000 cases of heart disease,

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:13.280
stroke, or cancer in the U.S. every year.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.680
It’s hard to get everyone to go&nbsp;
all kale and quinoa overnight,

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:20.000
but a veggie burger should be easy.

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:24.713
100,000 cases prevented every year,

00:19:24.713 --> 00:19:29.200
with a single daily serving&nbsp;
swap of plant-based meat.

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:32.240
Now, that’s just based on their&nbsp;
respective nutrient make-up—

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:35.040
like how much saturated fat&nbsp;
versus fiber they each had.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:38.240
But remember how all the harmful effects

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:43.760
of ultra-processed foods are not necessarily&nbsp;
captured by their nutrient profile?

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:47.520
There are 16 proposed mechanisms

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:51.440
linking ultra-processed&nbsp;
foods to disease and death,

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.200
and balance of nutrients is only one of those 16.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:58.080
And so, using these 16 criteria,

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:01.440
normally, when you compare&nbsp;
ultra-processed products

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:03.200
to the foods they were designed to replace,

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:06.560
for example, water vs. Kool-Aid,

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:09.600
or fruit vs. fruit-flavored candy,

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:11.920
the ultra-processed product tends to do worse

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:16.640
and not just because of the&nbsp;
junkier nutrient profile.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:21.920
However, plant-based meats&nbsp;
appear to be the exception,

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:28.640
better in most ways compared to the&nbsp;
foods they were designed to replace.

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:30.640
We already covered nutrient profile,

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:34.480
where plant-based meat tended to&nbsp;
score healthier than regular meat,

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:35.760
based on every study,

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.880
using every major nutrient profiling system.

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:41.680
I don’t have time to go&nbsp;
through all of these right now.

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:43.120
For those who want dive a little deeper,

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:45.360
I had a paper published this year on the subject,

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:47.040
which you’re welcome to download.

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:50.160
This Thursday, the 21st,

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:53.040
I’m going to be giving a&nbsp;
three-hour webinar on the subject,

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:56.960
where I will go into detail through&nbsp;
all the rest of those criteria,

00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:59.920
and I’ve got a book coming out on ultra-processed

00:20:59.920 --> 00:21:00.960
that will be out in January;

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:03.440
so, you’ll have all the references handy,

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:04.880
but with limited time,

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:07.200
I’m going to skip through a bunch of these,

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:10.400
though happy to talk about them in Q&amp;A.

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:12.800
In terms of additives,

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:17.840
I’ve been long concerned that Red&nbsp;
No. 3 hadn’t been banned from food,

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:21.360
given that it was banned over 30 years ago

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:26.080
from anything going on our&nbsp;
skin, due to cancer risk.

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:29.520
But it’s still ok to eat?

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:32.800
Thankfully, California passed a Food Safety Act,

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:35.840
set to ban Red Dye No. 3 by 2027.

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:46.080
So, in 2027, Loma Linda Big Franks&nbsp;
will be illegal to sell in Loma Linda

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:49.520
but would still be available to&nbsp;
potentially contribute to cancer risk

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:52.160
throughout the rest of the country.

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:56.160
But, the FDA finally decided to ban it from food,

00:21:56.800 --> 00:21:59.520
35 years after it removed it from cosmetics,

00:21:59.520 --> 00:22:01.440
starting in 2027 as well.

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:08.800
So, for now, this veggie bacon is&nbsp;
considered too toxic to rub on your skin

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:10.160
but okay to eat—

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:14.400
at least, for the next two years.

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:20.000
Why has the pork bacon industry&nbsp;
figured out how to use cherry powder

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:28.400
as a coloring before the&nbsp;
plant-based bacon industry?

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:33.120
The most harmful additive in&nbsp;
plant-based meat, is ironically,

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.680
really the most traditional and that’s salt,

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:39.680
contributing to the #1 dietary risk&nbsp;
factor for death on planet Earth,

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:41.680
excess sodium consumption.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:45.520
It is one of the most common ingredients&nbsp;
in plant-based meat alternatives.

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:47.960
But, also one of the most frequent ingredients

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:49.840
used in conventional meat processing,

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:51.840
and not just in processed meat.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:56.320
While the #1 source of sodium for&nbsp;
kids in this country is pizza,

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:58.640
and in older individuals bread,

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:01.200
one of the reasons the #1 source of sodium

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:04.640
for those aged 19 through 50 in this country

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:11.120
is chicken is because injecting&nbsp;
raw chicken with saltwater

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:15.120
is a widespread practice in the poultry industry,

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:17.200
something they’ve been doing since the 1970s,

00:23:17.200 --> 00:23:22.160
resulting in raw chicken breasts reaching&nbsp;
as much as 400mg of sodium or more,

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:25.760
six times more than they’d naturally have.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:30.000
When it comes to salt, how does&nbsp;
meat and plant-based meat compare?

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:33.320
A systematic review found that&nbsp;
the average levels of sodium

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:35.280
were not significantly different overall

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.200
between plant-based and conventional meat,

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:38.960
which is not great,

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:41.600
but there are some products are improving.

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:44.560
When I created a chart five years ago for UBS,

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:49.840
Beyond Meat burgers had 390 milligrams of sodium,

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:56.400
which is in the typical 290 to 400&nbsp;
range of a regular American hamburger,

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:02.320
but the current version is 20% lower at 310 mg,

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:04.320
thanks to the use of potassium salt,

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:07.040
potassium chloride instead of sodium chloride,

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:11.840
and Impossible now has a&nbsp;
beef “lite,” with only 260.

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:14.880
Ideally, we would be under 180,

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:17.760
meeting the World Health&nbsp;
Organization’s recommendation

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:21.520
for less than a 1:1 ratio of sodium to calories.

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:26.880
All the foods we eat ideally should&nbsp;
have less sodium than calories.

00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:30.640
Plant-based meat tends to be better&nbsp;
when it comes to some additives,

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:33.680
like carcinogenic nitrite preservatives,

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:38.000
similar with salt and sugar,&nbsp;
and worse with emulsifiers,

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:42.080
which might be expected to&nbsp;
hurt the gut microbiome,

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:45.600
but vegetarians who eat plant-based meat

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:48.960
actually end up with lower rates&nbsp;
of irritable bowel syndrome

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:50.080
than those who don’t,

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:52.560
which suggests that at least on that metric

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:54.880
the emulsifiers don’t seem to be a problem.

00:24:54.880 --> 00:24:59.040
In fact, all three studies on&nbsp;
the impact of plant-based meat

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:02.000
show microbiome benefits;

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:03.920
for example, this randomized, controlled trial

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:06.000
analyzing stool samples before and after

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:10.400
even just swapping out about&nbsp;
five meals of meat a week

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:12.080
for plant-based meat,

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:15.760
confirming that even just the&nbsp;
occasional replacement of meat

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:19.680
may promote positive changes&nbsp;
in the gut microbiome.

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:22.560
In the second trial, the Mycomeat study,

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:25.600
people were randomized to replace&nbsp;
a few servings of meat a day

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:28.480
with the mycoprotein meat Quorn—

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:31.360
made from the mushroom kingdom&nbsp;
rather than the plant kingdom.

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:35.760
This swap not only increases the abundance&nbsp;
of beneficial microbes in the gut,

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:39.120
it reduces fecal genotoxicity,

00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:44.480
which is the amount of DNA damage that&nbsp;
their feces can cause colon cells.

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:47.440
Now, that may just have been because&nbsp;
they were cutting down on meat,

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:51.120
but on the Quorn, there was&nbsp;
a boost in good bacteria,

00:25:51.120 --> 00:25:55.520
like lactobacillus, which may have benefits,

00:25:55.520 --> 00:26:00.960
suggesting that the fiber in mycoprotein&nbsp;
may have prebiotic potential.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:04.920
The two-week swap did not&nbsp;
seem to be long enough time

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:07.680
to affect TMAO levels, though,

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:10.960
but in the 8-week Stanford SWAP-MEAT study,

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:13.360
in which two or more servings&nbsp;
a day of beef, pork, or chicken

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:16.720
were swapped out with plant-based&nbsp;
beef, pork, or chicken,

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:19.120
several cardiovascular risk factors improved,

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:23.520
including TMAO, which stands&nbsp;
for trimethylamine n-oxide,

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:29.920
considered like the smoking gun in&nbsp;
gut microbiome-disease interactions.

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:35.120
When we eat meat, dairy, or eggs,

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:39.840
the carnitine and choline get turned by&nbsp;
certain bad gut bugs into trimethylamine,

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:42.320
which is oxidized by our liver into TMAO

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:46.960
which can go on to damage various&nbsp;
organ systems throughout the body.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:51.120
So, the cardiovascular harm from eggs and meat

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:54.000
is more than just cholesterol and saturated fat.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:57.840
TMAO is linked to some of our deadliest diseases—

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:02.560
cardiovascular disease, diabetes,
high blood pressure, stroke, cancer.

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:09.920
Check out those P values.

00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:13.280
Those, are my kind of P values.

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:18.720
Like six times ten to the negative 21st.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:22.240
That means you’d only get a&nbsp;
finding that extreme by chance

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:26.560
if you ran the study about&nbsp;
a billion trillion times.

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:34.560
So, TMAO may also mean time to&nbsp;
minimize intake of animal products,

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:39.680
which we can do by switching to plant products.

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:43.280
Plant-based meals can also be better&nbsp;
for the upper digestive tract.

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:47.280
If you randomize people to eat&nbsp;
chicken, cheese, fish, and steak,

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:51.600
versus tofu, soy steaks, seitan, and soy burgers,

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:55.680
you see twice as much heartburn&nbsp;
after the animal protein meal

00:27:55.680 --> 00:27:58.000
than after the plant-protein meal.

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:02.080
And stick a probe down their throats,&nbsp;
and after the animal products,

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:07.040
you see three times as much acid&nbsp;
exposure refluxing up from their stomach

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:09.600
into their esophagus.

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.240
The bottom line is that,

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:15.360
unlike almost any other ultra-processed product,

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:18.000
plant-based meat appears to be the rare case

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:21.680
in which ultra-processed products&nbsp;
are actually better, overall,

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:24.800
than the foods they were designed to replace.

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:28.560
Now, this probably says less how healthy they are

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:31.360
and more about how really&nbsp;
unhealthy modern meat is.

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:34.560
Take food safety, for example.

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:36.800
In response to a Super Bowl ad

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:40.400
placed by a meat-industry-funded lobbying group

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:46.160
questioning plant-based meat for&nbsp;
its hard-to-spell ingredients,

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:49.280
Impossible Foods responded with a parody,

00:28:49.280 --> 00:29:00.320
questioning an unintentional&nbsp;
additive in conventional meat.

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:04.320
Fecal contamination of the&nbsp;
carcass in the slaughter plant

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:06.960
is considered unavoidable,

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:13.040
and though there are methods for removing&nbsp;
visible fecal contamination on meat,

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:15.760
and even experimental imaging technologies

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:20.000
designed to detect more&nbsp;
“diluted fecal contaminations,”

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:25.360
we’re still left at a retail level&nbsp;
with guess what proportion of meat

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:29.200
contaminated with fecal&nbsp;
bacteria at the grocery store?

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:34.640
About 85% of ground beef and turkey&nbsp;
contaminated with fecal bacteria,

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:39.040
and about half of chicken on store&nbsp;
shelves, and a third of pork,

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:41.200
found contaminated with Enterococci,

00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:46.720
which is used as a marker of&nbsp;
fecal contamination of food.

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:50.800
But you don’t have to cook the&nbsp;
crap out of plant-based meat,

00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:55.920
because there shouldn’t be any crap to begin with.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:02.240
Fecal contamination is not just unseemly&nbsp;
but a critical public health issue.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:05.200
Fecal matter is considered the main source

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:07.120
of some of our most serious foodborne pathogens

00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:09.600
like Salmonella and Campylobacter.

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:13.600
Salmonella is the leading cause of foodborne&nbsp;
hospitalizations in the United States

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:16.240
and the #1 cause of foodborne death,

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:21.680
and 1 in 4 packages of chicken sampled&nbsp;
in the United States by the USDA

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:24.240
contaminated with Salmonella,

00:30:24.240 --> 00:30:27.520
1 in 8 packages of ground turkey,
1 in 24 packages of pork,

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:29.760
and 1 in 67 packages of ground beef,

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:33.040
and 1 in 4 packages of retail chicken.

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:39.480
Campylobacter, which sickens more&nbsp;
than 800,000 Americans a year,

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:44.240
is also found in every 1 in 3 or&nbsp;
1 in 4 packages of retail chicken.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:47.920
Even just shopping for meat can be risky,

00:30:47.920 --> 00:30:52.720
as disease-causing fecal bacteria can&nbsp;
contaminate the outer packaging of meat.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:58.720
But, the good news is that food-borne&nbsp;
illnesses are largely preventable,

00:30:58.720 --> 00:31:02.160
and the simplest approach&nbsp;
to reduce their occurrence

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:06.320
is to greatly reduce the consumption&nbsp;
of animal products like meat.

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:08.320
They’re intestinal bugs,

00:31:08.320 --> 00:31:10.880
and since plants don’t have any intestines,

00:31:10.880 --> 00:31:13.600
we can reduce the risk with plant-based meat,

00:31:14.560 --> 00:31:16.720
though there are always cases of&nbsp;
manure run-off contaminating crops.

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:25.040
But the leading causes of large food-borne&nbsp;
outbreaks of food poisoning are all meat,

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:29.040
and every single one of the&nbsp;
top pathogen-food combinations

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:32.240
that cause the greatest burden of&nbsp;
disease are all animal products,

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:33.920
mostly meat.

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:36.080
That second one is toxoplasma,

00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:42.720
which is a brain parasite that may&nbsp;
infect a million Americans a year,

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:48.320
making it a leading source of severe&nbsp;
foodborne illness in the United States.

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:54.960
What may end up in your brain&nbsp;
may start in your burger.

00:31:54.960 --> 00:31:58.160
Tapeworms in your brain from pork

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:01.760
have become an increasingly important&nbsp;
emerging infection in the United States.

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:08.720
What you think is a migraine&nbsp;
may not be a migraine.

00:32:08.720 --> 00:32:11.040
This is what they found in the brain

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:20.800
of our current Secretary of&nbsp;
Health and Human Services.

00:32:20.800 --> 00:32:24.800
You may even get bladder&nbsp;
infections from eating meat,

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:27.680
particularly poultry, urinary tract infections,

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:32.400
or viral hepatitis from pork&nbsp;
products—hepatitis E infection.

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:35.360
There aren’t any prions in plants either,

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:38.240
the cause of fatal spongiform brain diseases,

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:41.360
which is why there’s a mad cow disease,

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:45.120
but not a mad Quorn disease.

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:50.560
And I didn’t include other meat-borne pathogens,

00:32:50.560 --> 00:32:53.360
like trichinosis or E. coli O157:H7,

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:57.120
which still sickens 50,000&nbsp;
Americans a year, mostly from beef,

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:01.840
or the 100,000 sickened by&nbsp;
yersinia-infected pork every year,

00:33:01.840 --> 00:33:04.160
but let’s just stick to the major pathogens,

00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:08.880
some of which can become antibiotic-resistant.

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:12.240
Consuming contaminated meat with&nbsp;
antibiotic-resistant bacteria

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:16.160
is considered a severe public health hazard.

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:21.280
To help compensate for the overcrowded,&nbsp;
stressful, unhygienic conditions,

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:25.200
animals raised for food are&nbsp;
dosed en masse with antibiotics.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:28.240
Lots of antibiotics. This is in kilos;

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:36.000
so, we’re talking 10 million pounds a&nbsp;
year of medically-important antibiotics.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:38.560
So, we give farm animals in the United States

00:33:38.560 --> 00:33:42.000
more than a million pounds&nbsp;
of penicillin drugs a year.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.800
Four thousand tons of tetracyclines.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:50.880
Millions of pounds of drugs, laced&nbsp;
right into their food and water.

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:53.440
Antibiotics important to human medicine

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:56.160
being fed to cows, pigs, chickens by the ton—

00:33:56.160 --> 00:34:00.080
by the thousands of tons every year.

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:02.720
And so now in chicken breasts, for example,

00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:05.920
most of the Salmonella and&nbsp;
Campylobacter found is now resistant

00:34:05.920 --> 00:34:07.560
to at least one class of antibiotics,

00:34:07.560 --> 00:34:10.720
and about half of the Salmonella is resistant

00:34:10.720 --> 00:34:13.520
to three or more classes of drugs.

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:18.320
Even organic meat raised without antibiotics

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:20.800
can be contaminated with&nbsp;
multidrug-resistant bacteria,

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:23.520
often because there is cross-contamination&nbsp;
in the slaughter plant.

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:26.960
It’s funny—in the cover story of Consumer Reports

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:30.320
that urged retailers to stop selling&nbsp;
meat produced with antibiotics,

00:34:30.320 --> 00:34:34.080
they noted, some store employee confusion:

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:35.680
“An assistant manager at one store,

00:34:35.680 --> 00:34:39.520
when asked by a shopper for&nbsp;
meats raised without antibiotics,

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:49.520
responded, ‘Wait, you mean,&nbsp;
like, veggie burgers?'” Exactly.

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:52.960
Antibiotic-resistance genes can&nbsp;
also be transmitted through meat,

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:55.840
which can then transfer to&nbsp;
other pathogens in our gut,

00:34:55.840 --> 00:34:59.520
as well as residues of the&nbsp;
antibiotic drugs themselves,

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:02.640
which is itself considered a&nbsp;
serious public health threat.

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:05.520
You can correlate how much meat people eat;

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:07.920
you can just measure the amount of antibiotics

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:10.880
flowing out of their urine every day.

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:15.600
These drug residues in the&nbsp;
meat can cause allergies,

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:18.480
nerve damage, liver damage,&nbsp;
reproductive disorders,

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:21.760
bone marrow toxicity, even increased cancer risk,

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:26.880
not to mention what eating antibiotics&nbsp;
every day is doing to your gut microbiome.

00:35:27.760 --> 00:35:30.800
In contrast, the production of&nbsp;
plant-based meat involves no guts,

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:34.400
no feces, no antibiotics necessary.

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:38.640
In addition to antibiotics, livestock&nbsp;
may be fed or injected with hormones.

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:41.200
Currently, seven hormone&nbsp;
drugs are approved by the FDA

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:42.880
to bulk up the production of milk and meat.

00:35:42.880 --> 00:35:48.000
In Europe, there exists a&nbsp;
total ban on all such use.

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:50.320
But even without injected hormones,

00:35:50.320 --> 00:35:52.240
animal products naturally contain hormones

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:55.520
because they come from animals.

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:58.480
I already touched on processing contaminants,

00:35:58.480 --> 00:36:01.920
but there are also industrial contaminants&nbsp;
that build up in the food chain,

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.480
like certain pesticides, PCBs, heavy metals,

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:08.000
and ﬂame retardant chemicals that&nbsp;
end up concentrating in meat.

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:11.440
Can’t you just choose organic meat?

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:14.720
Surprisingly, the consumption of organic meat

00:36:14.720 --> 00:36:18.000
does not seem to diminish&nbsp;
the carcinogenic potential

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:21.520
associated with these industrial pollutants.

00:36:21.520 --> 00:36:24.560
If you look at the micropollutants&nbsp;
and chemical residues

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:28.320
in conventional vs. organic meat,

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:32.720
several environmental contaminants—like&nbsp;
dioxins, PCBs, lead, arsenic

00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:37.760
sometimes measured at signiﬁcantly&nbsp;
higher levels in organic meat,

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:40.640
though cooking does draw off some of the fat

00:36:40.640 --> 00:36:43.440
where the PCBs are concentrated.

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:45.760
Seafood seems to be the exception, though,

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:47.040
with steaming, for example,

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:51.920
generally increasing the concentration&nbsp;
of contaminants like mercury.

00:36:51.920 --> 00:36:58.560
There is, however, no mercury&nbsp;
added to plant-based tuna.

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:01.280
When researchers tested retail meat

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:05.600
for the presence of 33 chemicals with&nbsp;
calculated carcinogenic potential,

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:12.320
like organochlorine pesticides&nbsp;
like DDT and dioxin-like PCBs,

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:15.600
they concluded in order to&nbsp;
reduce our risk of cancer,

00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:18.720
we should limit our ingestion&nbsp;
of beef, pork, or chicken

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:23.920
to a maximum of five servings—a month.

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:27.360
Meat presents the only scenario

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:30.560
in which ultra-processed products&nbsp;
were designed to replace foods

00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:32.880
that we know cause cancer,

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:37.520
in fact, the #1 cancer killer of&nbsp;
nonsmokers, colorectal cancer.

00:37:38.240 --> 00:37:43.200
These are foods in desperate need of replacement.

00:37:43.200 --> 00:37:46.960
Processed meat—bacon, ham,&nbsp;
hot dogs, lunch meat, sausage—

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:50.720
is considered a known human carcinogen,

00:37:50.720 --> 00:37:54.400
leading major cancer centers, like Mount Sinai,

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:57.280
Memorial Sloan Kettering, and MD Anderson,

00:37:57.280 --> 00:38:01.520
to recommend flat-out avoid processed meat.

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:05.120
All in all, plant-based meat is&nbsp;
better than animal-based meat

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:06.800
in about 40 different ways.

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:11.040
Now obviously, some of these categories&nbsp;
are more important than others.

00:38:11.040 --> 00:38:15.360
The fact that bacon is officially&nbsp;
designated as cancer-causing

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:19.120
is more important than the fact&nbsp;
that it may have a few more calories

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:20.960
than plant-based bacon.

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:23.360
But cancer is just killer #2.

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:28.560
The leading cause of death in&nbsp;
men and women is heart disease,

00:38:28.560 --> 00:38:30.480
and hidden in that nutrient profile,

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:32.880
are the three things that raise LDL cholesterol:

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:35.680
saturated fat, trans fat, and dietary cholesterol.

00:38:35.680 --> 00:38:39.440
Yes, there are other factors,&nbsp;
like TMAO and sodium.

00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:41.960
But LDL cholesterol, also&nbsp;
known as bad cholesterol,

00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:46.061
is unequivocally recognized&nbsp;
as the principal driver

00:38:46.061 --> 00:38:48.560
in the development of atherosclerotic&nbsp;
cardiovascular disease,

00:38:48.560 --> 00:38:52.000
our leading cause of death.

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:59.840
Basically, we should try to&nbsp;
get our LDL as low as possible.

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:04.000
When it comes to LDL lowering for&nbsp;
the prevention of heart disease,

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:06.480
“Lower for longer is better.”

00:39:06.480 --> 00:39:10.480
Even if your LDL is quote-unquote normal;

00:39:10.480 --> 00:39:14.400
even if other heart disease risk&nbsp;
factors are considered optimal,

00:39:14.400 --> 00:39:18.840
still, of utmost importance to control it.

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:23.600
So, if LDL is the primary&nbsp;
driver of our primary killer,

00:39:23.600 --> 00:39:25.840
if you could just know one thing about a food,

00:39:25.840 --> 00:39:27.920
if could just ask one question,

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:32.640
it would be “What does this&nbsp;
food do to my LDL cholesterol?”

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:36.560
So, we’re looking at nutrition labels for&nbsp;
saturated fat, trans fat, and cholesterol,

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:41.280
since any intake above zero increases&nbsp;
LDL cholesterol and, therefore,

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:44.160
increases our risk of coronary heart disease.

00:39:44.160 --> 00:39:46.080
In terms of saturated fat and cholesterol,

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:49.120
animal meats are the main&nbsp;
source in the American diet,

00:39:49.120 --> 00:39:54.240
and with the removal of partially&nbsp;
hydrogenated oils from the U.S. food supply,

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:58.880
such that these other sources are now banned,

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:02.000
animal products are now the&nbsp;
leading source of trans fat as well

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:06.080
(since meat and dairy&nbsp;
naturally contain trans fat).

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:11.200
Online, some bloggers parrot&nbsp;
egg industry propaganda

00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:16.640
that the 2015 U.S. Dietary Guidelines&nbsp;
removed its dietary cholesterol limit,

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:21.040
whereas if anyone actually&nbsp;
bothered to read the guidelines,

00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:23.840
they’d see that the guidelines actually&nbsp;
strengthened their recommendation,

00:40:23.840 --> 00:40:29.760
telling Americans to “eat as little&nbsp;
dietary cholesterol as possible,”

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:32.000
as recommended by the Institute of Medicine,

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:35.280
the most prestigious medical&nbsp;
body in the United States.

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:38.720
This advice was reiterated in the&nbsp;
subsequent dietary guidelines:

00:40:38.720 --> 00:40:40.880
“The National Academies recommends

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:46.160
that dietary cholesterol consumption&nbsp;
to be as low as possible.”

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:49.760
While eggs are the most concentrated&nbsp;
source of cholesterol gram-for-gram,

00:40:49.760 --> 00:40:53.760
the greatest cholesterol contribution&nbsp;
in the overall American diet is meat,

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:55.760
including poultry and fish.

00:40:55.760 --> 00:40:58.640
Since cholesterol is present&nbsp;
only in animal-derived foods,

00:40:58.640 --> 00:41:01.200
it’s not surprising that plant-based&nbsp;
meat contains little or no cholesterol

00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:03.840
(unless they have cheese&nbsp;
or something added in it).

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:09.200
And saturated fat content is&nbsp;
usually low in plant-based meat

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:12.560
compared to regular meat, averaging&nbsp;
to one half and one third.

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:14.880
You can see that illustrated graphically here,

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:19.440
where the conventional meat is&nbsp;
those orange bars across the top,

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:23.920
so better across the board,&nbsp;
often by a large margin.

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:27.920
This is as true in Europe as well&nbsp;
as in South Africa and the U.S.—

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:33.440
where plant-based meat may only&nbsp;
average a third of the saturated fat.

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:36.880
Swapping out meat for plant-based meat&nbsp;
significantly lowers saturated fat,

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:40.000
but does this translate into lower LDL?

00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:41.600
That’s really what we care about.

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:48.880
Well, you don’t really know,&nbsp;
until you put it to the test.

00:41:48.880 --> 00:41:53.360
Here’s a meta-analysis of 10&nbsp;
randomized controlled trials

00:41:53.360 --> 00:41:56.800
replacing some or all meat with&nbsp;
plant-based or mycoprotein-based meats,

00:41:56.800 --> 00:41:58.640
and within an average of six weeks,

00:41:58.640 --> 00:42:01.360
saw a 15-point drop in LDL cholesterol.

00:42:01.360 --> 00:42:03.680
What does that mean in terms of disease risk?

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:08.000
Maintaining a 15-point drop in LDL for five years

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:10.920
would be expected to decrease your&nbsp;
risk of heart disease by about 10%,

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:13.840
and after a dozen years by more like 15%,

00:42:13.840 --> 00:42:18.640
and across a lifetime by more like 25%.

00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:22.640
And risk reduction is independent of baseline LDL,

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:25.920
meaning you get about the&nbsp;
same relative risk reduction,

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:30.080
even if your LDL is so-called&nbsp;
normal, less than 100.

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:36.320
One would also expect the use of egg&nbsp;
substitutes to reduce cardiovascular risk,

00:42:36.320 --> 00:42:39.200
given that eggs are the most concentrated&nbsp;
source of dietary cholesterol.

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:44.240
What about substituting soymilk for cow’s milk?

00:42:44.240 --> 00:42:46.800
Based on 17 randomized, controlled trials,

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:48.480
drinking soymilk instead of cow’s milk

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:52.000
resulted in significant&nbsp;
improvements in blood pressure,

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:54.960
in cholesterol, in inflammation,

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:55.670
an 8-point drop in systolic blood pressure,

00:42:55.670 --> 00:42:58.080
a 5-point drop in diastolic blood pressure,

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:03.680
a 7-point drop in LDL&nbsp;
cholesterol switching to soy,

00:43:03.680 --> 00:43:05.520
as well as a drop in c‑reactive protein,

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:07.200
so less inflammation as well.

00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:08.720
Over a lifetime,

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:13.360
that 7-point drop in LDL could drop our&nbsp;
risk of heart disease by more than 10%,

00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:18.960
just making a single dietary swap from cow to soy.

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:22.400
Now, the benefit only accrues&nbsp;
if your cholesterol stays down.

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:25.040
And it turns out, it may even get better.

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:27.760
In a four-year study in which&nbsp;
diabetics were randomized

00:43:27.760 --> 00:43:31.920
to swap out half their animal protein&nbsp;
for ultra-processed plant protein,

00:43:31.920 --> 00:43:34.960
their LDL went down year after year,

00:43:34.960 --> 00:43:38.800
ending up 26 points lower than the control group.

00:43:38.800 --> 00:43:43.520
Whoa, that could net you a nearly&nbsp;
40% drop in risk, over a lifetime,

00:43:43.520 --> 00:43:49.040
of our #1 killer, just swapping out&nbsp;
half of their animal protein servings.

00:43:49.040 --> 00:43:54.160
An estimated 20 million&nbsp;
Americans have heart disease,

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:58.560
having about 800,000 heart attacks a year.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:02.400
If we started swapping our animal&nbsp;
products for plant products,

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:07.440
imagine how many lives could be saved&nbsp;
by even a 10% drop in heart disease.

00:44:07.440 --> 00:44:09.760
Instead of swapping out a&nbsp;
burger for a veggie burger,

00:44:09.760 --> 00:44:12.400
why not just switch to chicken and fish?

00:44:12.400 --> 00:44:15.520
Because, it doesn’t work.

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:17.600
A meta-analysis of randomized, controlled trials

00:44:17.600 --> 00:44:23.360
that compared the cholesterol effects&nbsp;
of beef versus poultry-and/or-ﬁsh

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:26.640
found no significant difference in terms of LDL.

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:29.600
And an updated meta-analysis&nbsp;
of four times as many studies

00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:33.600
also found no benefit to&nbsp;
switching to chicken and poultry,

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:37.440
and the beef was actually&nbsp;
found to be better than fish,

00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:41.520
which they attribute to using particularly&nbsp;
lean cuts of beef as the comparator,

00:44:41.520 --> 00:44:44.880
particularly because the beef industry&nbsp;
is the one that funded these studies.

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:51.520
But even organic, grass-fed beef appeared&nbsp;
to be no match for plant-based meat,

00:44:51.520 --> 00:44:56.880
as shown in the Stanford SWAP-MEAT trial.

00:44:56.880 --> 00:45:00.320
With processed meat officially&nbsp;
classified as a known human carcinogen,

00:45:00.320 --> 00:45:05.120
global health organizations flat-out&nbsp;
recommend avoid processed meat,

00:45:05.120 --> 00:45:08.240
but looking at six health outcomes—

00:45:08.240 --> 00:45:10.720
colorectal cancer, breast cancer,

00:45:10.720 --> 00:45:12.400
ischemic heart disease, type 2 diabetes,

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:14.000
and two types of stroke—

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:19.600
the optimal amount of unprocessed&nbsp;
red meat may be zero as well,

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:22.400
but even a 30% reduction in both types of meat

00:45:22.400 --> 00:45:25.280
could lead to a million fewer&nbsp;
cases of type 2 diabetes,

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:27.600
hundreds of thousands of fewer&nbsp;
cases of cardiovascular disease,

00:45:27.600 --> 00:45:32.240
and tens of thousands fewer cases of&nbsp;
cancer and premature death over a decade.

00:45:32.240 --> 00:45:35.120
Diets high in processed and red meat

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:39.360
are also the two single leading causes of&nbsp;
diet-related disability in the United States,

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:43.760
responsible for more than a million&nbsp;
years lived in disability annually—

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:49.200
three times more than diets high in&nbsp;
sugar-sweetened beverages, for example,

00:45:49.200 --> 00:45:53.280
and more than two million&nbsp;
years of healthy life lost,

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:55.910
based on the most comprehensive&nbsp;
analysis of risk factors

00:45:55.910 --> 00:45:57.680
for death and disability in history.

00:45:57.680 --> 00:46:00.240
Millions of years of lost&nbsp;
life every year in the U.S.

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:03.840
because people are eating diets&nbsp;
high in red and processed meat.

00:46:04.560 --> 00:46:07.760
What do they define as high, though?

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:16.720
A diet “high” in processed meat is&nbsp;
defined as any intake of processed meat.

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:19.520
And a diet high in red meat?

00:46:19.520 --> 00:46:24.800
High as in any intake of red meat.

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:28.080
So, millions of years of life&nbsp;
lost in the United States,

00:46:28.080 --> 00:46:32.400
because people are eating more&nbsp;
than zero red and processed meat.

00:46:32.400 --> 00:46:33.920
Higher intake of meat in general—

00:46:33.920 --> 00:46:37.280
red meat, white meat, processed, unprocessed—

00:46:37.280 --> 00:46:42.160
also associated with an increased risk&nbsp;
of death from all-causes put together.

00:46:42.160 --> 00:46:44.720
If people swapped out 75% of their meat,

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:49.200
up to 50,000 lives a year could&nbsp;
be saved in high income countries.

00:46:49.200 --> 00:46:53.760
What would happen if the&nbsp;
whole world cut out all meat?

00:46:54.880 --> 00:47:00.880
We would save an estimated 7 million lives a year,

00:47:00.880 --> 00:47:05.200
and if humanity cut out all animal products,

00:47:05.200 --> 00:47:09.680
we might annually save 8 million lives.

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:12.000
Hanging in the balance!

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:17.360
The bottom line is that veggie&nbsp;
burgers are not Twinkies,

00:47:17.360 --> 00:47:19.920
even though they’re both classified&nbsp;
as ultra-processed foods.

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:24.000
Now, there are two directions&nbsp;
we can go with that fact.

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:28.160
We can decry the very concept&nbsp;
of ultra-processed foods.

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:31.360
Or we can take what I would&nbsp;
argue is a better tact:

00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:34.080
keep the baby; throw out the bathwater.

00:47:34.080 --> 00:47:38.480
In other words, not deny&nbsp;
ultra-processed foods tend to be worse,

00:47:38.480 --> 00:47:40.880
not deny that plant-based&nbsp;
meats are ultra-processed,

00:47:40.880 --> 00:47:43.600
but rather that despite this classification,

00:47:43.600 --> 00:47:45.960
plant-based meats and milks are the rare exception

00:47:45.960 --> 00:47:49.040
in that they compare well with the&nbsp;
foods they were designed to replace,

00:47:49.760 --> 00:47:53.520
in some cases, potentially&nbsp;
even saving thousands of lives.

00:47:53.520 --> 00:47:55.760
But there is some merit to the argument

00:47:55.760 --> 00:48:00.720
that the ultra-processed concept is&nbsp;
not as useful as many people think.

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:03.840
If you look at the three big Harvard cohorts—

00:48:03.840 --> 00:48:05.680
200,000 people followed for decades.

00:48:05.680 --> 00:48:09.840
Yes, total ultra-processed foods intake&nbsp;
was associated with cardiovascular risk,

00:48:09.840 --> 00:48:11.680
but if you break it down,

00:48:11.680 --> 00:48:17.520
the risk is driven exclusively by soda and meat.

00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:18.560
Sugar-sweetened beverages,

00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:20.400
a little from artificially sweetened beverages,

00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:23.840
and the rest from processed meat, poultry, fish.

00:48:23.840 --> 00:48:26.400
If you exclude soda and meat,

00:48:26.400 --> 00:48:30.640
the relationship between ultra-processed&nbsp;
food and cardiovascular disease

00:48:30.640 --> 00:48:32.960
disappears completely.

00:48:32.960 --> 00:48:35.840
So, nutritional advice for cardiovascular health

00:48:35.840 --> 00:48:37.760
should consider the differential consequences

00:48:37.760 --> 00:48:40.800
of group-speciﬁc ultra-processed foods,

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:45.120
and speciﬁcally, that means&nbsp;
processed meats and soft drinks

00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:46.880
should be discouraged.

00:48:46.880 --> 00:48:49.040
What about mortality?

00:48:49.040 --> 00:48:51.754
Yes, those who ate the most&nbsp;
ultra-processed foods in general

00:48:51.754 --> 00:48:53.680
had a higher risk of dying prematurely,

00:48:53.680 --> 00:48:56.080
but it mattered what kind&nbsp;
of ultra-processed foods,

00:48:56.080 --> 00:48:59.440
with ready-to-eat meat,&nbsp;
poultry, and seafood products

00:48:59.440 --> 00:49:02.560
showing particularly strong&nbsp;
associations with mortality.

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:05.040
The apparent worst ultra-processed food

00:49:05.040 --> 00:49:08.080
when it comes to dying prematurely in general?

00:49:08.080 --> 00:49:10.000
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:13.520
The worst when it comes to dying from cancer?

00:49:13.520 --> 00:49:14.800
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:18.400
The worst when it comes to dying&nbsp;
from cardiovascular disease?

00:49:18.400 --> 00:49:19.440
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:23.360
The worst when it comes to dying&nbsp;
from lung diseases like emphysema?

00:49:23.360 --> 00:49:24.400
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:49:24.400 --> 00:49:28.320
The worst when it comes to dying&nbsp;
from neurodegenerative diseases?

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:30.080
(Ice cream).

00:49:30.080 --> 00:49:34.480
And the worst when it comes&nbsp;
to dying from other causes?

00:49:34.480 --> 00:49:37.120
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:49:37.120 --> 00:49:39.120
Bottom line,

00:49:39.120 --> 00:49:41.760
the major factors contributing&nbsp;
to the harmful effects

00:49:41.760 --> 00:49:47.680
of ultra-processed foods on mortality are&nbsp;
processed meat, poultry, fish, and soda.

00:49:47.680 --> 00:49:51.600
So, the negative, life-shortening&nbsp;
effects of ultra-processed food

00:49:51.600 --> 00:49:54.160
is really mostly talking about the&nbsp;
negative, life-shortening effect

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:58.240
of ultra-processed meat like burgers,&nbsp;
chicken nuggets, and fish sticks.

00:49:59.200 --> 00:50:02.560
Ideally, we would have a study that&nbsp;
specifically looked at meat alternatives.

00:50:02.560 --> 00:50:06.000
And, here we go: a quarter-million&nbsp;
people followed for over a decade,

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:08.640
and while the higher consumption&nbsp;
of ultra-processed foods in general

00:50:08.640 --> 00:50:12.160
increases the risk of cancer and&nbsp;
cardiovascular disease and diabetes,

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:17.600
but that was driven by ultra-processed&nbsp;
animal products and soda,

00:50:17.600 --> 00:50:20.160
not plant-based meats.

00:50:20.160 --> 00:50:24.400
They specifically studied plant-based&nbsp;
alternatives and found no increased risk.

00:50:24.400 --> 00:50:26.960
And when it comes to diabetes,

00:50:26.960 --> 00:50:32.640
plant-based meat and milk appears to cut&nbsp;
the risk of developing diabetes in half.

00:50:33.280 --> 00:50:38.080
Animal-based products were associated&nbsp;
with more than twice the risk;

00:50:38.080 --> 00:50:42.400
plant-based alternatives with less than half.

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:44.800
So, when the authors conclude&nbsp;
that higher consumption

00:50:44.800 --> 00:50:47.840
of ultra-processed foods may increase&nbsp;
the risk of cancer and other diseases,

00:50:47.840 --> 00:50:50.640
their data really only show

00:50:50.640 --> 00:50:54.880
that the consumption of foods&nbsp;
of animal origin and soft drinks

00:50:54.880 --> 00:50:56.640
is associated with such a risk,

00:50:56.640 --> 00:50:59.600
and indeed, when the authors were challenged

00:50:59.600 --> 00:51:03.120
to go back and exclude animal foods and beverages,

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:09.200
the relationship between ultra-processed&nbsp;
foods and multiple diseases disappears.

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:13.600
That’s also what a systematic review and&nbsp;
meta-analysis of all such studies showed.

00:51:13.600 --> 00:51:18.240
The only categories of ultra-processed&nbsp;
foods associated with higher mortality

00:51:18.240 --> 00:51:22.560
were sweetened beverages and meat.

00:51:23.200 --> 00:51:23.680
So, wait.

00:51:23.680 --> 00:51:28.480
If the only ultra-processed food that&nbsp;
appears to be killing people is meat,

00:51:28.480 --> 00:51:30.960
then in that case, instead of&nbsp;
being a part of the problem,

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:36.480
plant-based meats may be the solution&nbsp;
to the ultra-processed problem.

00:51:37.120 --> 00:51:38.000
Let me say that again;

00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:39.200
instead of being a part of the problem,

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:43.920
plant-based meats may be the solution&nbsp;
to the ultra-processed problem.

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:47.520
The one other ultra-processed study&nbsp;
that separated out plant-based meats

00:51:47.520 --> 00:51:51.440
looked at telomere length, which&nbsp;
is used to measure cellular aging.

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:55.040
Their study found that a higher&nbsp;
consumption of total ultra-processed foods

00:51:55.040 --> 00:51:56.880
was associated with a shorter telomere length,

00:51:56.880 --> 00:51:58.480
a sign of accelerated aging.

00:51:58.480 --> 00:52:01.200
However, some subclasses of ultra-processed foods

00:52:01.200 --> 00:52:03.600
were associated with longer telomere length,

00:52:03.600 --> 00:52:05.120
suggesting slower aging.

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:09.600
And, the class of foods associated&nbsp;
with the longest telomeres?

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:13.760
Vegetarian alternatives: plant-based meat.

00:52:13.760 --> 00:52:19.040
The most important question&nbsp;
in all of nutrition may be,

00:52:19.040 --> 00:52:20.240
“Compared with what?”

00:52:20.240 --> 00:52:25.120
The effects of any given food depend on&nbsp;
what that food is being compared against.

00:52:25.120 --> 00:52:26.720
So, is plant-based meat healthy?

00:52:26.720 --> 00:52:29.040
Compared to the animal products&nbsp;
they were intended to replace?

00:52:29.040 --> 00:52:30.720
Absolutely.

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:34.720
Plant-based meat alternatives are more&nbsp;
healthful than the meat they replace,

00:52:34.720 --> 00:52:37.600
but perhaps less so than whole plant foods,

00:52:37.600 --> 00:52:39.200
such as legumes and whole grains.

00:52:39.200 --> 00:52:42.800
Wait, just perhaps less so?

00:52:42.800 --> 00:52:46.000
This is how ultra-processed&nbsp;
plant meat compares to meat.

00:52:46.000 --> 00:52:51.840
This, is how unprocessed plants compare to meat.

00:52:51.840 --> 00:52:54.000
Not only making up for all the shortfalls,

00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:56.560
but if we add an even better category,

00:52:56.560 --> 00:53:00.560
whole plant foods do even&nbsp;
better, in many categories.

00:53:00.560 --> 00:53:05.920
For example, bean-based meat averages&nbsp;
five times less saturated fat

00:53:05.920 --> 00:53:07.440
than their meat comparators,

00:53:07.440 --> 00:53:11.440
but actual beans have 40 times less:

00:53:12.880 --> 00:53:17.680
half the trans fat or no trans fat.

00:53:17.680 --> 00:53:20.160
Five grams of fiber is certainly better than zero,

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:23.760
but nine grams is even better.

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:27.760
And there are interventional trials&nbsp;
demonstrating the greater benefits.

00:53:27.760 --> 00:53:31.440
For example, better at fighting&nbsp;
insulin resistance and inflammation.

00:53:31.440 --> 00:53:35.280
So certainly, whole foods for the win,

00:53:35.280 --> 00:53:37.280
but plant-based meat alternatives,

00:53:37.280 --> 00:53:40.080
they are not designed to&nbsp;
replace whole plant foods,

00:53:40.080 --> 00:53:44.640
instead to offer a steppingstone&nbsp;
in the transition away from meat.

00:53:44.640 --> 00:53:47.280
I think of it like meat methadone.

00:53:52.240 --> 00:53:54.160
Or, as Professor Gardner put it,

00:53:54.160 --> 00:53:59.440
“I’m hoping that plant-based meats&nbsp;
will be the gateway drug to legumes.”

00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:06.640
This is how the transition to&nbsp;
healthier diets is envisioned,

00:54:06.640 --> 00:54:11.760
from the global burden of disease,
to the global relief of disease,

00:54:11.760 --> 00:54:16.000
as people use plant-based&nbsp;
and mycoprotein-based meats

00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:21.200
to transition toward a more&nbsp;
whole food, plant-based diet.

00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:23.360
With the added saturated fat and sodium,

00:54:23.360 --> 00:54:27.440
plant-based meats are not&nbsp;
healthy—but, they are healthier.

00:54:27.440 --> 00:54:30.240
They are healthier than the foods&nbsp;
they were designed to replace.

00:54:30.240 --> 00:54:33.040
They are better, but not the best.

00:54:33.040 --> 00:54:37.440
Yes, nutrition policies and dietary guidelines,

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:40.000
the bottom line is that we should&nbsp;
continue to emphasize a diet

00:54:40.000 --> 00:54:41.280
rich in whole plant foods.

00:54:41.280 --> 00:54:45.840
Indeed, if all meat in high income&nbsp;
countries were replaced with whole legumes,

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:49.760
we could potentially decrease&nbsp;
overall mortality rates by 5 or 6%.

00:54:49.760 --> 00:54:55.120
That means saving 180,000 lives&nbsp;
in the United States every year.

00:54:55.120 --> 00:55:00.720
If instead, we replaced meat with&nbsp;
processed or ultra-processed plants,

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:05.040
we’d only save about 130,000 Americans a year

00:55:05.040 --> 00:55:07.440
(about a 4% drop in overall mortality).

00:55:07.440 --> 00:55:12.080
That’s still over 100,000 people saved every year.

00:55:12.080 --> 00:55:16.080
So, we shouldn’t let the perfect&nbsp;
be the enemy of the good.

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:20.480
Plant-based meat alternatives&nbsp;
are better than the alternative.

00:55:20.480 --> 00:55:23.040
Or as one study put it,

00:55:23.040 --> 00:55:28.480
plant-based meat can be&nbsp;
considered “meat with benefits.”

00:55:28.480 --> 00:55:31.577
Thank you so much.

00:55:55.984 --> 00:55:58.800
Wow, what a way to end the conference.

00:55:58.800 --> 00:56:00.880
That was absolutely phenomenal.

00:56:00.880 --> 00:56:08.720
I think he deserves another round of applause.

00:56:08.720 --> 00:56:12.480
All right, Dr. Greger, do you&nbsp;
have time for two questions?

00:56:12.480 --> 00:56:13.600
Let's do it.

00:56:13.600 --> 00:56:15.120
All right.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:18.080
So, on one of the slides that you just ended on,

00:56:18.080 --> 00:56:19.760
there was, right at the bottom,

00:56:19.760 --> 00:56:23.760
it spoke about mold toxins being&nbsp;
higher in plant-based meats.

00:56:23.760 --> 00:56:25.520
Could you comment on that?

00:56:25.520 --> 00:56:27.680
Yeah. So there's mycotoxins.

00:56:27.680 --> 00:56:30.160
So, I have a bunch of videos on mycotoxins

00:56:31.040 --> 00:56:39.120
that affect the majority of the&nbsp;
food crops grown in the world today.

00:56:39.120 --> 00:56:41.280
And although, you know,

00:56:41.280 --> 00:56:43.360
animals that eat contaminated moldy food crops

00:56:43.360 --> 00:56:47.920
can build up these mycotoxins like aflatoxin,

00:56:49.120 --> 00:56:53.120
the plant products tend to&nbsp;
have higher levels in general.

00:56:54.240 --> 00:56:57.840
And so the concern is—

00:56:57.840 --> 00:57:00.480
so there was a modeling study in Europe

00:57:00.480 --> 00:57:07.760
that suggested that if everybody switched&nbsp;
from their current meat consumption

00:57:07.760 --> 00:57:12.000
to 100% plant-based meat,

00:57:12.000 --> 00:57:18.560
the mycotoxin exposure, the increased&nbsp;
mycotoxin exposure that they get,

00:57:18.560 --> 00:57:22.160
based on, you know, studies of&nbsp;
hundreds of plant-based products,

00:57:22.160 --> 00:57:36.080
would cause, between one in one million&nbsp;
or one in two million more cases

00:57:36.080 --> 00:57:41.280
of liver cancer because of the aflatoxin content.

00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:50.720
So, one in one or two million&nbsp;
people would get liver cancer

00:57:50.720 --> 00:57:54.080
that wouldn’t have gotten liver cancer&nbsp;
if they had stuck with the meat.

00:57:54.080 --> 00:57:59.680
Of course, the amount of colorectal&nbsp;
cancer that would be prevented

00:57:59.680 --> 00:58:06.720
by switching to plant-based&nbsp;
meat would far overwhelm that,

00:58:06.720 --> 00:58:10.240
you know, one million cases&nbsp;
or one in two million cases.

00:58:10.240 --> 00:58:12.880
Having said that, it's&nbsp;
still not acceptable, right?

00:58:12.880 --> 00:58:15.920
I mean, so these plant-based companies—

00:58:15.920 --> 00:58:19.360
so I encourage—so in the book I talk&nbsp;
about how really they should be tested.

00:58:19.360 --> 00:58:20.240
You can certainly—

00:58:20.240 --> 00:58:23.280
I mean, you can test for, you know, your feed,

00:58:23.280 --> 00:58:26.480
the, you know, the soy or whatever&nbsp;
you're using in your products,

00:58:26.480 --> 00:58:28.880
and you just make sure that&nbsp;
they don't reach levels

00:58:28.880 --> 00:58:31.920
that could cause any harm, right?

00:58:31.920 --> 00:58:34.640
We don't just want overwhelming benefit over harm.

00:58:34.640 --> 00:58:36.640
How about overwhelming benefit with no harm?

00:58:36.640 --> 00:58:42.080
And so, unfortunately, mycotoxins is one&nbsp;
of the things that is actually lower—

00:58:42.080 --> 00:58:43.200
the concern is lower in meat—

00:58:43.200 --> 00:58:48.640
but still overwhelming for trying&nbsp;
to prevent cancer overwhelmingly.

00:58:48.640 --> 00:58:52.080
But that doesn't put plant-based&nbsp;
meat companies off the hook,

00:58:52.080 --> 00:58:54.640
and I encourage them to start testing to make sure

00:58:54.640 --> 00:58:58.880
that they indeed drop their levels lower.

00:58:58.880 --> 00:59:03.600
And a question that actually stumped&nbsp;
Dr. Christopher Gardner earlier.

00:59:03.600 --> 00:59:04.640
Bring it.

00:59:05.280 --> 00:59:07.760
You have to answer in one word.

00:59:07.760 --> 00:59:09.360
One word?

00:59:09.360 --> 00:59:12.960
What is your favorite&nbsp;
plant-based ultra-processed food

00:59:12.960 --> 00:59:16.320
after all this research that you've done?

00:59:16.320 --> 00:59:17.360
He couldn't answer?

00:59:18.080 --> 00:59:19.600
It took him a while.

00:59:19.600 --> 00:59:23.440
Ah, what did he finally say?
Well, no, no, no. Don't, don't.

00:59:23.440 --> 00:59:25.920
Well, okay, this is actually controversial

00:59:25.920 --> 00:59:27.920
whether it's actually considered ultra-processed,

00:59:27.920 --> 00:59:33.200
but, so tempeh, some regard that&nbsp;
as an ultra-processed product

00:59:33.200 --> 00:59:35.520
because some—you can't really&nbsp;
make it in your home kitchen

00:59:35.520 --> 00:59:38.240
because they use these, you know, fancy processes.

00:59:38.240 --> 00:59:41.360
You actually can make tempeh,&nbsp;
but it's quite a process.

00:59:41.360 --> 00:59:43.520
And if you read the&nbsp;
instructions for making tempeh,

00:59:43.520 --> 00:59:45.760
it's like if there's black mold, that's fine.

00:59:45.760 --> 00:59:47.680
But if it's gray mold, then&nbsp;
you don't want, you know,

00:59:47.680 --> 00:59:49.360
it's like, I'll let you make it.

00:59:51.360 --> 00:59:56.000
But that's an example of a processed product

00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:58.080
that's actually healthier than the whole food.

00:59:58.080 --> 00:59:59.520
So, the whole food is soybeans,

00:59:59.520 --> 01:00:02.720
and then they process it&nbsp;
by adding these, you know,

01:00:02.720 --> 01:00:06.320
these micro-protein threads,&nbsp;
you know, this culture,

01:00:06.320 --> 01:00:08.160
and they ferment it into tempeh.

01:00:08.160 --> 01:00:09.120
So, it actually ends—

01:00:09.120 --> 01:00:13.040
it's the rare, it's a rare processed&nbsp;
product essentially healthier

01:00:13.040 --> 01:00:14.640
than just the regular whole food.

01:00:14.640 --> 01:00:17.440
It has more spermidine, more&nbsp;
ergothioneine, all sorts of things.

01:00:18.480 --> 01:00:23.040
So, I love that as like, you know, there's&nbsp;
a processed product I can get behind.

01:00:23.040 --> 01:00:25.920
You're actually processing&nbsp;
it to make it healthier,

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:29.920
and that's unfortunately all too rare.

01:00:29.920 --> 01:00:33.200
Dr. Gardner said soy milk.
Oh, not sure which one.

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:35.760
So, it was better.

